# Independent verification of the three-branch compose onto perf/p2-kernels

date: 2026-08-07
host: Apple M4 Pro (8 P-core + 4 E-core = 12 logical), macOS 26.5.2, aarch64
rustc: stable, release, DEFAULT features (bitdepth_8 + bitdepth_16, CHECKED
       DisjointMut, tracker armed). No `-C target-cpu=native`. No `nice` on any
       timed run.
tree:  verify/compose = perf/p2-kernels @ 0624dfd
         + perf/lf-neon-port  @ 9c5093e  (fast-forward)
         + perf/cdef-neon     @ ad52dcd  (3 doc/script conflicts, resolved)
         + perf/p3-t8-inversion @ 761366e (1 CHANGELOG conflict, resolved)
       All three merged. Nothing was left out.
vectors: ~/tmp/rav1d-perf/vec/v4k_8tile{,_10b}.avif (3840x2160, 4x2 tiles) and
         their IVF twins in ~/tmp/recon-yard/vec, plus the 766-vector
         dav1d-test-data corpus.
harness: scripts/perf/verify_gap.sh + scripts/perf/verify_gap_report.py (new,
         this campaign). Raw: benchmarks/verify_gap_2026-08-07.tsv.

This file records what an INDEPENDENT re-measurement found, not what the three
branch reports claimed. Where the two differ, the difference is stated.

## HEADLINE: the composed tree is NOT bit-identical to its baseline, and that is a FIX

The task framed any divergence from `perf/p2-kernels` as a hard stop. There is
one, at every 10-bit vector and every thread count, and it goes the right way.

Frame MD5, visible planes, 7 local vectors x t=1/2/4/8, against dav1d 1.5.4's
own `--muxer md5`:

  vector           dav1d      main      p2base    lf_only   cdef_only  composed
  v4k_8tile        a00c11f4   a00c11f4  a00c11f4  a00c11f4  a00c11f4   a00c11f4
  v4k_8tile_10b    4f218411   840d4364  840d4364  840d4364  4f218411   4f218411
  v4k_1tile_10b    9ed1be40   a913b26b  a913b26b  a913b26b  9ed1be40   9ed1be40
  v1024_10b        a3e14e71   da847dfb  da847dfb  da847dfb  a3e14e71   a3e14e71
  v1024            0a17a5eb   0a17a5eb  0a17a5eb  0a17a5eb  0a17a5eb   0a17a5eb
  v256             c5e7bbf1   c5e7bbf1  c5e7bbf1  c5e7bbf1  c5e7bbf1   c5e7bbf1
  v4k_1tile        472c34f6   472c34f6  472c34f6  472c34f6  472c34f6   472c34f6

`main` and `perf/p2-kernels` produce WRONG 10-bit pixels on aarch64. Root cause,
read from source and confirmed by the isolation arms above:

  src/cdef.rs:557                (scalar reference)  pri_strength >> bitdepth_min_8 & 1
  src/safe_simd/cdef.rs:1903/2116 (x86 16bpc)        pri_strength >> bitdepth_min_8 & 1
  src/safe_simd/cdef_arm.rs:777  (ARM 16bpc, main)   pri_strength & 1        <-- WRONG

`bitdepth_min_8` is 0 at 8bpc, which is why only the high-bit-depth ARM path is
affected and why the 8-bit hashes never moved. `perf/cdef-neon` carries the
one-line fix (issue #446 / PR #448) because its 16bpc vector kernel cannot be
bit-exact without it.

Independently reproduced on the full dav1d-test-data corpus
(`cargo test --release --test decode_md5_verify`, 766 vectors, same host, same
corpus, run on both trees back to back):

  distinct mismatching vectors      p2-kernels 556  ->  composed 464
  newly matching                                        92
  NEWLY FAILING (regressions)                            0

  per-suite deltas, all of them positive:
    10-bit/quantizer     3 passed -> 64 passed
    10-bit/data          0 passed -> 26 passed
    10-bit/film_grain    1 passed ->  6 passed
    every other suite    unchanged, to the vector

So the composed tree is not bit-identical to its baseline; it is bit-identical
to **dav1d** on 7/7 local vectors at 4 thread counts, where the baseline was
bit-identical to dav1d on only the 3 eight-bit ones. This is the single most
valuable thing in the compose and it is a correctness result, not a perf one.

(The `perf/cdef-neon` record already claims exactly 556 -> 464 / 0 / 92. That
claim is CONFIRMED by independent re-measurement.)

## Bit-inertness of the other two branches

* `perf/lf-neon-port` is byte-identical to the baseline on all 7 vectors at
  t=1/2/4/8 and at 8/10/12 bpc — its bit-identity claim holds. Note this means
  it is bit-identical to a baseline that is WRONG at 10 bpc; it neither fixes
  nor worsens that.
* `perf/p3-t8-inversion` is bit-inert: `kern` (LF+CDEF) and `all` (LF+CDEF+P3)
  produce identical hashes in all 28 grid cells. A tracker change should be
  bit-inert and this one is.

## Soundness, re-proved on the composed tree

* **`#![forbid(unsafe_code)]` is intact.** The default build has no `asm` /
  `c-ffi` / `partial_asm`, so the crate-root `cfg_attr` forbid is active, and it
  compiles. The only `unsafe` tokens the three-branch diff ADDS are two
  `feature = "asm"`-gated `extern "C"` FFI wrappers being RENAMED
  (`cdef_find_dir_{8,16}bpc_neon` -> `..._ffi`, because the new safe function
  took the old name). Net new `unsafe`: zero.
* **Threading suite, all green** (`--include-ignored`, release, this tree):
  `mt_stress_4k` at 1/2/4/8/16 threads; `tile_threading_overlap`
  (single_threaded_no_panic, multi_threaded_cdef_lpf_race,
  multi_threaded_tile_overlap); `tile_threading_parity` 3/3;
  `reproduce_overlap` 6/6 + `reproduce_overlap_scalar`; `panic_safety_test` 4/4;
  `worker_panic_recovery` 6/6; `thread_cleanup_test` 2/2; `cancellation` 6/6;
  `integration_decode` 9/9; lib unit tests 48/48.
* **Deliberate-overlap detection is still LIVE — proved by planting mutations,
  not by reading the passing test.** Two mutations were planted in
  `tracker_shard.rs` on the composed tree and both were caught by
  `crates/rav1d-disjoint-mut/tests/wide_exclusion.rs`:
  - **M1**, `active()` shortened to `&self.shards[..1]` (the exact hazard the
    P3 branch's narrowing introduces): FAILED with "a narrow mutable borrow was
    granted inside a wide mutable borrow that was provably still held".
  - **M2**, the 4af62ae TOCTOU re-read of `state` INSIDE the shard lock deleted
    from `add`: FAILED with the same assertion.
  Restored, re-ran, green. So the gate detects both the new hazard and the old
  one, and the TOCTOU shape has NOT been reintroduced — `add` still re-reads
  `state` under the lock (`tracker_shard.rs:848`).
* **Concurrent-decoder pressure, no overlap panic.**
  - `scripts/perf/p2_concurrent_overlap.sh` on this tree's
    `tile_threading_overlap` binary, 4 rounds x 6 processes: **0/24 FAILED**.
  - 36 simultaneous 4K decoders at t=8 (3 rounds x 6 8bpc + 6 10bpc): 0 nonzero
    exits, 0 occurrences of "overlapping"/"panic"/"DisjointMut" in any log, and
    all 18 8bpc runs agree on `a00c11f4...` while all 18 10bpc runs agree on
    `4f218411...` — i.e. the dav1d-matching hashes, under load.
* **Guard extents were not widened.** Read at every changed site:
  - CDEF `padding_8bpc`/`padding_16bpc` fold two per-row immutable guards
    (`[0,w)` then `[0,w+2)` in the HAVE_RIGHT loop) into one over
    `read_w = w + 2*HAVE_RIGHT`. Same maximum instantaneous extent, one fewer
    guard per row. Write guards are still `slice_mut(w)`.
  - Loop filter `LfBlock::fill::<W>` takes ONE immutable guard per picture row
    of exactly `W == w` pixels — the const only changes the copy's codegen.
    `LfBlock::close` still takes its mutable guard over the exact changed span
    from `changed_span`, which is what the P1 barrier removal depends on.
* **`set_parallelism` is monotone and instance-local.** `ACTIVE_SHARDS` only
  moves by `fetch_max` over {32, 128}, both powers of two; `mask_for` is read
  once in `BorrowTracker::new` and is immutable for that tracker's life;
  `shard_of` ends in `& self.mask`, so every index an instance produces is
  inside `0..=mask` — which is exactly the prefix `active()` holds. Sound.

## Pre-existing failures, unchanged by the compose (verified by A/B, not asserted)

* `decode_permutations`: 16/19 FAIL on BOTH `perf/p2-kernels` and the composed
  tree, same 16 test names, same panic sites — 14x
  `Arm64::summon().unwrap()` on `None` in `src/safe_simd/mc_arm.rs`, 2x the
  same in `filmgrain_arm.rs`. Those unwraps are pre-existing at 6 sites on
  `perf/p2-kernels`. This is a real panic-freedom defect on the aarch64
  MC/filmgrain fallback paths and it belongs to NEITHER of the three branches.
  (Note the test needs `--test-threads=1`; running it in parallel produces the
  same failures for an additional reason.)
* `decode_md5_verify`'s remaining 464 mismatching vectors — the documented
  pre-existing aarch64 set, owned by other kernels.
* `mt_stress` needs `test-vectors/bench/photo_4k.avif`, which is not in the
  worktree unless you symlink it; it FAILS LOUD rather than skipping. Good.

## The gap to dav1d, re-measured on an IDLE box in one session

`scripts/perf/verify_gap.sh`, 7 rounds, arms interleaved back to back with the
order rotating each round, STRICT idle guard (6 cells discarded and re-run; every
committed cell has foreign-process count 0). Wall clock at 2 and 20 frames with
`total = alpha + beta*frames` fitted, SAME instrument on dav1d and on us.
dav1d 1.5.4 (homebrew), measured BOTH at `--framedelay 1` (the tile-threading-only
model we implement) and at its shipping default. MEDIAN OF 7.

  arms:  base = perf/p2-kernels @ 0624dfd
         kern = base + lf-neon-port + cdef-neon (no tracker change)
         all  = base + all three (the composed tree)

  ms/frame          t     all     base    kern   dav1d_def  dav1d_fd1
  v4k_8tile         1   400.3    412.1   382.2      246.5      246.5
  v4k_8tile         2   223.8    260.6   242.8      125.7      125.2
  v4k_8tile         4   131.1    175.9   166.1       63.9       65.6
  v4k_8tile         8   116.5    187.8   179.2       34.7       37.4
  v4k_8tile_10b     1   504.8    600.4   490.0      251.3      251.5
  v4k_8tile_10b     2   275.0    357.1   296.0      128.8      127.6
  v4k_8tile_10b     4   158.4    225.2   188.8       65.4       66.8
  v4k_8tile_10b     8   142.4    214.2   195.0       35.8       37.9

  ratio to dav1d --framedelay 1
  v4k_8tile         1    1.62     1.67    1.55
  v4k_8tile         2    1.79     2.08    1.94
  v4k_8tile         4    2.00     2.68    2.53
  v4k_8tile         8    3.12     5.02    4.79
  v4k_8tile_10b     1    2.01     2.39    1.95
  v4k_8tile_10b     2    2.16     2.80    2.32
  v4k_8tile_10b     4    2.37     3.37    2.83
  v4k_8tile_10b     8    3.76     5.65    5.15

  t=1 -> t=8 scaling   all 3.44x / 3.54x   base 2.19x / 2.80x   dav1d_fd1 6.59x / 6.64x
  dav1d's own default is within 7% of its --framedelay 1 figure at every cell
  (0.93-1.01x), so the like-for-like restriction is not what the 3-5x is made of.

**Instrument cross-check.** This harness re-measures `base` at 412.1 / 260.6 /
175.9 / 187.8 against the 412.5 / 261.1 / 175.7 / 188.4 on record from the P2
campaign's different instrument (max deviation 0.32%), and dav1d at 246.5 /
125.2 / 65.6 / 37.4 against 246.7 / 125.1 / 65.6 / 36.0 (max 3.9%, at the t=8
cell where the absolute is 36 ms and the fit is most sensitive). The two
campaigns are directly comparable.

## The t=4 -> t=8 inversion is GONE, and the kernels alone did NOT fix it

  t=8 / t=4 (>1.0 means more threads made it slower)
                    all     base    kern    dav1d_fd1
  v4k_8tile       0.889    1.068   1.079        0.570
  v4k_8tile_10b   0.899    0.951   1.033        0.567

`kern` — the two kernel branches with the ORIGINAL 32-shard tracker — still
inverts at 8bpc (1.079) and now inverts at 10bpc too (1.033) where the baseline
did not (0.951). That is the expected direction: making the kernels cheaper
raises the tracker's share of the multi-thread wall. The P3 branch's claim that
"the tracker was the whole of the inversion" is CONFIRMED, and composing the
kernels without it would have made the inversion worse, not better.

t=8 is now the best thread count on the composed tree at both depths, as it
should be. It is still 5.4x off dav1d's own t=1 -> t=8 scaling (3.44x vs 6.59x).

## Attribution: which branch bought what, and is the tracker change worth keeping

Second sweep, same harness, 8 arms interleaved, 5 rounds, IDLE-BOX, ZERO cells
discarded. Raw: `benchmarks/verify_attrib_2026-08-07.tsv`.

  arms:  vlf = base + lf-neon-port only     vcd = base + cdef-neon only
         kern = both kernels, 32 shards     s32/s64 = composed + shards-32/-64
         all = composed (128 shards, the shipped default)

  ms/frame           base    vlf    vcd   kern    s32    s64    all   dav1d_fd1
  8bpc  t=1         412.5  384.1  404.7  381.7  385.7  388.1  400.5      246.6
  8bpc  t=8         187.2  185.3  182.8  181.3  179.5  151.2  117.2       36.2
  10bpc t=1         601.0  571.8  519.7  489.9  493.6  496.8  505.7      251.6
  10bpc t=8         213.2  209.8  199.9  194.6  193.9  160.9  137.8       38.1

  isolated speedup vs base    8bpc t=1   8bpc t=8   10bpc t=1   10bpc t=8
  loop filter (vlf)             1.074x     1.010x      1.051x      1.016x
  CDEF (vcd)                    1.019x     1.024x      1.156x      1.067x

**Both kernel branches are LIVE and both self-reported speedups reproduce.** The
LF branch claimed 1.074x at 8bpc t=1 and this measures 1.074x. The CDEF branch
claimed 1.019x at 8bpc t=1 and 1.154x at 10bpc t=1; this measures 1.019x and
1.156x. Those were the two claims most at risk of being a dispatch that never
fires, and they are not.

**The shard count decomposes cleanly, and 128 is the right pick.**

  from kern (32 shards, no P3)     8bpc t=1        8bpc t=8
  + wide-path narrowing + latch     +1.0%  385.7    -1.0%  179.5   (= s32)
  + 64 shards                       +1.7%  388.1   -16.6%  151.2
  + 128 shards (shipped)            +4.9%  400.5   -35.4%  117.2

  s64 buys 47% of the t=8 win for 34% of the t=1 cost — strictly worse than 128
  on both counts of the trade. There is no gating question: keep 128.

**The P3 branch's admitted +4.2% single-thread regression is real, is +4.9% once
composed on the faster kernels, and it does NOT make the composed tree net
negative anywhere.** `all` at t=1 is 400.5 against base's 412.5 (2.9% FASTER) at
8 bpc and 505.7 against 601.0 (1.19x faster) at 10 bpc. The regression is only
visible against `kern`, an arm nobody proposes to ship.

## MEMORY: a cost the P3 branch did not report

`/usr/bin/time -l` max RSS, one 4K decode, 3 iterations, v4k_8tile 8bpc:

  arm     t=1        t=8
  base    79.4 MiB   83.7 MiB
  kern    79.5 MiB   83.8 MiB
  s32     79.5 MiB   83.7 MiB
  s64     86.3 MiB   91.5 MiB
  all     99.3 MiB  106.1 MiB

**The 128-shard default costs +19.8 MiB at t=1 and +22.3 MiB at t=8 — about
+25% of peak RSS for a single 4K decode.** That follows straight from 16 KiB per
`BorrowTracker` instead of 4 KiB, times the number of live `DisjointMut`s
(`Rav1dTaskContext` embeds ~20 of them per worker). It is not a reason to
withhold the change, but it is a real trade and the P3 record does not mention
memory at all. An embedder running many concurrent decoders should know.

## What was NOT verified

* `TinyLock` spin backoff measured null — NOT re-measured here; the
  `probe-lock-backoff` arm was not built. Taken on the P3 record's word.
* The "28 -> 20 borrows per CDEF block" count was checked by READING the guard
  sites (two per-row immutable guards folded into one of the same maximum
  extent), not by running a `probe-count` build.
* 12 bpc has no end-to-end local vector, as the LF branch admits. The
  dav1d-test-data 12-bit suites DO exercise it end to end, and their pass/fail
  sets are identical on base and composed (12-bit/data 1 passed / 45 failed on
  both), so the port introduced no 12-bit change — but "no change" is not
  "correct", and 45 of those 46 vectors were already failing.
* No x86_64 build or run. Everything here is aarch64.
* `cargo clippy` and `cargo fmt` were not run on the composed tree.
